; Regression: an argument that names NOTHING used to travel without a word
; through a static call (2026-08-15).
;
; `Box.twice(nosuchname, 3)` planned with rc 0 and passed whatever the argument
; slot happened to hold. The unknown name gets a fresh type variable, which
; matches anything, so nothing downstream objected either.
;
; The three call shapes disagreed about this, which is what made it hard to
; recognise as one class:
;
;   free function   `twice(nosuchname, 3)`   reported "function not found:
;                                            twice" — the right file and line,
;                                            the wrong subject
;   static / method `Box.twice(nosuchname)`  reported nothing at all
;
; The check now sits where every call shape collects its arguments (the s90
; block in collapse_operand_sequence#), so all three say the same true thing and
; name the typo rather than the callee.
;
; Cost of the old behaviour: a mechanical rename left
; `Primitive.grove_entity(gnode, …)` in a body with no `gnode` in scope, and the
; result was a SIGSEGV three suites away.
;
; xfail: unknown identifier: nosuchname

define Box
(
    v: int
)
{
    function twice(a: int, b: int) returns int
    {
        a * 2 + b
    }
}

let r Box.twice(nosuchname, 3)
print "unreachable"
